---
title: Authentication
description: Sign in from your terminal, or run an operator's own agent with client credentials.
estimate: 3 min
section: SDK
order: 2
---

A client finds its credentials on its own, in this order:

1. `KEELSON_CLIENT_ID` and `KEELSON_CLIENT_SECRET`: an operator's agent, with OAuth client credentials.
2. `KEELSON_TOKEN`: a token you already hold.
3. The sign-in `keelson login` saved.

`KEELSON_API_URL` points a client at another deployment of the API; it defaults to `https://api.keelson.finance`.

## Sign in from your terminal

{% languages label="Sign in" %}
```sh {% title="TypeScript" %}
npx keelson login
```

```sh {% title="Python" %}
keelson login
```
{% /languages %}

From a clone of the repository, the same command is `node packages/sdk/dist/cli.js login`, or `uv run --project packages/sdk-python keelson login`.

Your browser opens Keelson's sign-in page, with a passkey or with Judge Mode, which needs no account. The CLI registers itself as an OAuth client for your machine, signs in with an authorization code and PKCE on a loopback redirect ([RFC 8252](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8252)), and saves the sign-in to `~/.config/keelson/credentials.json`, readable by you only. Both SDKs read the same file and refresh the token when it expires. There is no key to copy.

The sign-in may ask for mandates and buy under them:

| Scope | Lets the client |
| --- | --- |
| `mandates:request` | Ask the user for a mandate, or for a change to one |
| `evidence:write` | Seal what the agent saw before a purchase |
| `purchases:write` | Ask the two TEEs to countersign a cart |
| `alibis:read` | Read the Alibi of each purchase |

`keelson whoami` shows the user and scopes you are signed in with; `keelson logout` deletes the saved sign-in.

{% languages label="Use the sign-in" %}
```ts
import { Keelson } from "@keelson-finance/sdk";

const keelson = new Keelson(); // reads the saved sign-in
```

```python
from keelson import Keelson

keelson = Keelson()  # reads the saved sign-in
```
{% /languages %}

## An operator's own agent

An operator that runs its own agent registers an OAuth client and sets `KEELSON_CLIENT_ID` and `KEELSON_CLIENT_SECRET`. The client asks for `countersign:write`, `evidence:write` and `alibis:read`, and keeps the token until 30 seconds before it expires.

`KEELSON_AGENT_KEY` is the agent's private P-256 key, as a JWK. With it, the agent closes each mandate it buys under with its own signature. Without it, Keelson's hosted operator closes the mandate for the signed-in user and, by default, backs at most $100 per user for an app it does not know. In Python, install the `operator` extra to sign with the key.

{% languages label="Client credentials" %}
```ts
import { clientCredentials, Keelson } from "@keelson-finance/sdk";

const keelson = new Keelson({
  auth: clientCredentials({ clientId: process.env.CLIENT_ID!, clientSecret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET! }),
});
```

```python
from keelson import Keelson, client_credentials

keelson = Keelson(auth=client_credentials(client_id=client_id, client_secret=client_secret))
```
{% /languages %}

## A token you already hold

{% languages label="Static token" %}
```ts
import { Keelson, staticToken } from "@keelson-finance/sdk";

const keelson = new Keelson({ auth: staticToken(token) });
```

```python
from keelson import Keelson, static_token

keelson = Keelson(auth=static_token(token))
```
{% /languages %}

`auth: null` (`auth=None` in Python) makes a client that calls only the public operations, such as reading a public Alibi.
